FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/matrix.rs:390-406 — `matrix.matmul` checks only the inner dimensions, never the size of the result, so two operands each exactly at `MAX_ELEMENTS` produce an astronomically larger output. `matrix.ones(1000000, 1)` and `matrix.ones(1, 1000000)` both pass `element_count` (1,000,000 elements, the limit is `> 1_000_000`), then `left.dot(&right)` tries to allocate a `1e6 x 1e6` result (~8 TB), aborting the process. — Fix: call `element_count(span, left.nrows(), right.ncols())?` before `left.dot`. — VERIFIED: traced `element_count` at src/matrix.rs:52-67 (`elements > MAX_ELEMENTS` only), `ones` at 281-285 allowing exactly 1e6 per operand, and matmul at 394-406 checking only `left.ncols() != right.nrows()` before `value_of(span, &left.dot(&right))`.

2. [BLOCKER] src/matrix.rs:52-67 and 171-186 — `MAX_ELEMENTS` bounds only `rows * cols`, not the dimensions themselves, so a zero-column matrix with an unbounded row count is accepted: `matrix.zeros(100000000, 0)` has 0 elements and passes, then `say`/`matrix.to_lists` iterates `for row in 0..rows` a hundred million times. `matrix.transpose` of an `0 x N` matrix creates the same shape. This is an unbounded loop/OOM reachable from one Redblue expression, not a caught `Runtime` error. — Fix: reject `rows > MAX_ELEMENTS` and `cols > MAX_ELEMENTS` (including transposed results) in `element_count`, or cap dimension lengths separately. — VERIFIED: traced `element_count` computing `rows.checked_mul(cols)` where `cols == 0` yields 0 and the limit guard cannot fire, then `text` at src/matrix.rs:172-180 looping `0..rows` and formatting each row.

3. [MAJOR] src/matrix.rs:371-373 — the zero-divisor refusal branch in `matrix.divide` has no test on either side. `tests/matrix_test.rs:130-159` exercises `matrix.divide` only with mismatched shapes (which fail at `same_shape` first), and `tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs:695-698` divides `ones` by `ones`; nothing feeds a zero divisor, so deleting the guard would leave the suite green. — Fix: add a test asserting `matrix.divide(matrix.ones(2,2), matrix.zeros(2,2))` is a `Runtime` error naming the zero divisor, and that a non-zero divisor still divides.

4. [MINOR] SURVEY.md:16 — the stated dotted-member total is 138, but `MODULE_MEMBERS` contains 137 entries (the previous 117 plus the 20-member `matrix` row: 117 + 20 = 137, not 138). — Fix: change 138 to 137.

5. [MINOR] tests/matrix_test.rs:244-261 — the `m[row]` path added at src/runtime.rs:147-148 is only exercised through `eval`, which is the tree-walker; `edge_both_engines_produce_equal_matrix_data` (tests/matrix_test.rs:284-304) never indexes a matrix, so SPEC.md's claim that `m[row]` equals `matrix.row(m, row)` is unpinned on the bytecode VM. — Fix: add `m[1]` to the both-engines test so the runtime index branch is covered on both engines.

